//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// One SKU's memory hierarchy, for `vxc --machine fleet/arc-a770.vx prog.vx`.
// See fleet/README.md for the shared space vocabulary and provenance policy.
//
// The first Intel GPU here, and the first SKU whose device has no f64 at all:
// the card's arithmetic is f32/f16 and integer, and the driver offers no
// double-precision path either (`cl_khr_fp64` is absent from the OpenCL device
// and `shaderFloat64` is false under Vulkan). `dtypes:` therefore omits f64,
// and placing an f64 tensor here is E6026 before anything runs. That is the
// policy docs/gpu_backends.md states -- a device limitation is a compile error,
// never a silent change -- and this file is its first discrete-GPU case.
//
// Device execution: none yet. `arch:` names the device-image target a backend
// would emit (docs/gpu_backends.md, PR #1138); no such backend exists, so a
// region spawned on this topology takes the host fallback, or the honest
// refusal when its operands are in a space the host cannot read.
//
// Figures measured on the card 2026-10-04 (Level Zero, OpenCL and Vulkan
// queries) unless a `spec:` line names a published number.

// spec: Intel Arc A770 (16 GB), DG2-512 / ACM-G10 -- 16 GB GDDR6 on a 256-bit
// bus. TechPowerUp's GPU database gives the memory clock as 2000 MHz
// (16 Gbps effective), which is 256 bits x 16 Gbps / 8 = 512 GB/s; Intel's own
// product page quotes the same part, and if the board runs its memory at
// 17.5 Gbps the figure is 560 GB/s instead. UNVERIFIED against Intel's
// datasheet: the rendered spec table did not yield a readable figure, and a
// memory bandwidth is what derives every transfer cost on this SKU's edges.
//
// Measured: what the card reaches of it is a device-to-device copy at
// 449 GB/s and a triad at 422 GB/s (scripts/tools/xpu_bandwidth_spike.sh),
// the fraction of peak those two traffic shapes normally carry.
//
// Measured: the driver reports 16225243136 bytes (15.11 GiB) of device memory,
// about 0.9 GiB below the 16 GiB the part carries. Following fleet/README.md,
// `capacity:` is what the hardware has, not what a deployment may use, so the
// reservation is recorded here and not folded in. The check that would reject a
// working set between the two numbers is the same open one as #285.
Memory HBM {
  capacity: 16 GiB, bandwidth: 512 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// Measured: 16777216 bytes (16 MiB) reported as the device's global memory
// cache. Modelled as a device-scoped space within HBM, hardware-managed, so
// `cached`. No bandwidth is declared: none is published for this part, and
// leaving it off makes a route that has to cross L2 unpriceable rather than
// priced by a guess (fleet/README.md calls that a gap to close, and
// utils/memalg is how).
Memory L2 {
  within: Memory::HBM, capacity: 16 MiB, managed: cached, scope: device
}

// Measured: 65536 bytes (64 KiB) of workgroup shared local memory, the ceiling
// one workgroup may request; the maximum workgroup size is 1024 invocations
// with a preferred multiple of 64. Both are the numbers a backend has to
// respect, and both are API-visible: Vulkan on this same card reports 48 KiB,
// so the declared figure is the hardware's and the API's smaller one is a
// backend detail.
//
// No `replicas:` and no `clock:`. Which unit a workgroup's scratchpad belongs
// to on Xe (Xe-core, subslice, slice) is not settled by anything measured here,
// and `replicas:` divides a derived bandwidth, so a wrong value moves every
// on-die cost on this SKU by that factor.
Memory SMEM {
  within: Memory::L2, capacity: 64 KiB, managed: explicit, scope: sm
}

Topology Device {
  // The device-image format, not the die: Xe-HPG's ISA is not an LLVM target
  // Vx can name, and SPIR-V through Level Zero is the route docs/gpu_backends.md
  // records for Intel XPU. A Vulkan backend would emit the same format.
  arch: spirv64,
  memory: Memory::HBM,
  // Host memory is deliberately absent, as in fleet/h100-sxm.vx: the host
  // cannot address this device's memory and a kernel here cannot address host
  // memory, so either direction is an explicit transfer and E6003 says so.
  visible: [Memory::HBM, Memory::L2, Memory::SMEM],
  // spec: the XMX matrix units take INT2, INT4 and INT8 (TechPowerUp's DG2-512
  // entry), and the scalar path is f32/f16 and integer. INT4 is therefore a
  // matrix-unit format here, as it is on Ampere, and `i4`/`u4` are declared for
  // the same reason a100-80 declares them. INT2 has no `ElementType` and is not
  // listed, the way TF32 is absent from every file.
  // bf16 is left out although the matrix units most likely take it: an element
  // type lists what a machine file can cite, and omitting one rejects a bf16
  // program that would probably have run, which is the safe direction. Adding
  // it is one token once a source says so.
  dtypes: [f32, f16, i4, u4, i8, u8, i16, u16, i32, u32, i64, u64, bool],
  // Measured: 10.8 GB/s, and it is this platform's edge, not the card's. The
  // A770 is a Gen4 x16 part, so the datasheet figure is 31.5 GB/s per
  // direction, but this machine's CPU is a Ryzen 7 5700G (Cezanne) and its
  // lanes are PCIe 3.0 only, so the card runs at Gen3 x16: 15.75 GB/s of
  // theory, of which a copy through pinned host staging gets about two thirds.
  // Do not take the number back out of sysfs: `current_link_speed` and
  // `max_link_speed` at 0000:12:00.0 both report 2.5 GT/s x1 (Gen1 x1), which
  // cannot carry 10.8 GB/s, so this driver's link attributes are stale.
  // scripts/tools/xpu_bandwidth_spike.sh measures both directions.
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::HBM : 10.8 GB/s,
  // The way home, measured in the same run: 10.0 GB/s. Reading a placed tensor
  // back is how a program says it still holds it; without the edge the model
  // can only describe one direction, and fleet/mi300x.vx is the file that
  // currently cannot serve admission because it declares only the outbound hop.
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::CPU_DRAM : 10.0 GB/s,
  // The on-die hops declare no cost: HBM and L2 nest via `within:`, so the
  // roofline derives what their bandwidths support (E6013 forbids a second
  // source on one edge). No `copy_engine` on the scratchpad hop: whether a
  // global-to-SLM write is a copy-engine path on Xe or ordinary instruction
  // issue is unmeasured, and a capability asserted without a measurement is
  // what docs/memory_algebra.md records as falsified within hours on an A100.
  // Unmarked means `sequenced`, which costs the slowest leg rather than none.
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::L2,
  transfer Memory::L2 -> Memory::SMEM
}
